      "body": "> On timing I can argue only from the code. The output holds only the accepted `u` and `t`, and the rejected candidates are other SHA256 outputs of the same inputs. I don't see how they would reveal `seckey32` or `auxrnd32` without inverting SHA256, but I have not proven it.\n\nIndeed, this is not a security issue. The code is strictly speaking not constant-time time in `auxrnd32`, but branches happen on the resulting hash. The same is true for `seckey32`. The docs don't mention that `seckey32` and `auxrnd32` stay secret only against a computationally bounded timing attacker but this is fine.\n\nSo the issue is that the ctime tests don't cover `auxrnd32` (and adding it is not trivial because we need a precise declassification). I've edited the title to make this clear.\n\nSo how do we resolve this?\n\n1. Dropping the constant-time guarantee would be okay but dropping a guarantee doesn't sound like the ideal option (even though I can't see how anyone would rely on this guarantee in a meaningful way)\n2. Declassifying PRNG outputs sounds good though it adds complexity to the already complex declassification here.\n3. We could change the PRNG: We could create a `seed = seckey32 XOR SHA256(auxrnd32)` first (like in BIP340, maybe with a tagged hash), declassify `seed` and pass that one around to other functions instead of a hasher. (Or open a new hasher, write `seed` into it, and pass that one around.) This makes the declassification stuff simpler and doesn't even add a compression. Changing the PRNG is fine according to the header, though I'm not sure if it's worth doing it.\n\ncc @sipa ",
